  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: This afternoon and evening is that last\nday for highest chances (20-50%) for isolated to scattered wet/dry\nthunderstorms over northern and central Nevada. Tuesday and\nWednesday will still have chances for additional showers and\nthunderstorms, but at a lower chance (10-30%) and more across\neastern Nevada as the high pressure to the south moves west\ntowards Arizona, which will cut off the monsoon moisture flow.\nThunderstorms today will start off as dry then transition to a mix\nof wet and dry across central and northeastern Nevada, with\nHumboldt County expecting it by the evening. Storm motions is a\nlittle elevated which can hinder some storms from forming, however\nincreased amounts of moisture with PWATs at or above 0.8 and \nample instability will see rapid development with some of the\nstorms capable of producing heavy rainfall. Greatest threats from\nthese storms are still gusty erratic outflow winds up to 45-55\nmph, cloud to ground lightning strikes, and brief periods of \nheavy rainfall. Thursday through Sunday, models are showing better\nagreement for the weather pattern forming, with the upper level \nridge moving west, shifting the southerly flow into a more \nnorthwesterly flow with drier air. What has also changed with the \nmodels is they are now showing the upper ridge being further south\nover the US/Mex border with an upper level trough to the \nnorthwest settling over the Pacific ocean. This will cause a \nlongwave westerly flow over Nevada that will be dry, but with much\ncooler temperatures given the lower forecast heights. For Monday,\nthe models start to become different with each other but not by \nmuch, with both showing a trough forming over the northwest and \ncresting into Nevada, however ECMWF is showing the trough to be \nweak and short lived, while GFS is showing the trough to be \nstronger with the chance for more moisture. Either outcome will \nstill have much cooler temperatures for the day with a \nsouthwesterly flow present, yet is uncertain in regards to \nmoisture. Temperatures still on track with a cooling trend with \nhighs in the 90s the first half of the week and dropping into the \n80s over the weekend. Winds will be breezy in the afternoons with \nspeeds up to 10-15 mph, gusts 20-25 mph. These winds with drier \nair will cause some elevated fire weather concerns for Humboldt \nCounty as much of the monsoon activity has bypassed the area, \nleaving it drier than others.",
